INSERT INTO stories (id, date, month, summary_en, html_en, source_url, published) VALUES ('2019-04-18-ireland-b2g-mandate', '2019-04-18', '2019-04', 'Ireland mandated e-invoicing for Central Government Departments via the Peppol network from April 2019, using EN 16931 standards, with the Office of Government Procurement operating as Ireland''s Peppol Authority since January 2018.', '<h3>🇮🇪 B2G e-invoicing mandatory for Central Government (in force since April 2019)</h3>
<p>From this date, Ireland mandated structured e-invoicing for Central Government Departments through the Peppol network, using EN 16931 standards for exchange. The Office of Government Procurement (OGP) has operated as Ireland''s Peppol Authority since January 2018, running the technical and procurement infrastructure connecting Irish public bodies to the network, including a Multi-Supplier Framework Agreement giving public bodies access to Peppol Access Point services. By April 2020, this extended further: all public sector entities, not just Central Government, became required to receive and process e-invoices.</p>
<p>This B2G foundation matters beyond its own scope, since it''s the infrastructure Ireland''s much larger forthcoming domestic B2B mandate will build directly on top of — the same Peppol network, the same EN 16931 standard, and the same OGP-Revenue governance split, just extended to private-sector trading relationships rather than only public procurement.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> if you supply Irish Central Government or public bodies, this has been a settled requirement for years, not a new one — confirm your existing Peppol connectivity is genuinely still functioning correctly rather than assuming a years-old setup remains fine untested. Given this same infrastructure underpins the B2B mandate now being phased in, any gaps in your current B2G Peppol setup are worth resolving now, before the domestic B2B rollout adds real commercial pressure on top.</p>', 'https://www.revenue.ie/en/vat/vida-vat-modernisation/index.aspx', 1);
INSERT INTO story_countries (story_id, country_id) SELECT '2019-04-18-ireland-b2g-mandate', id FROM countries WHERE name_en = 'Ireland';
INSERT INTO story_translations (story_id, lang, title, summary, html) VALUES ('2019-04-18-ireland-b2g-mandate', 'en', 'B2G e-invoicing mandatory for Central Government (in force since April 2019)', 'Ireland mandated e-invoicing for Central Government Departments via the Peppol network from April 2019, using EN 16931 standards, with the Office of Government Procurement operating as Ireland''s Peppol Authority since January 2018.', '<h3>🇮🇪 B2G e-invoicing mandatory for Central Government (in force since April 2019)</h3>
<p>From this date, Ireland mandated structured e-invoicing for Central Government Departments through the Peppol network, using EN 16931 standards for exchange. The Office of Government Procurement (OGP) has operated as Ireland''s Peppol Authority since January 2018, running the technical and procurement infrastructure connecting Irish public bodies to the network, including a Multi-Supplier Framework Agreement giving public bodies access to Peppol Access Point services. By April 2020, this extended further: all public sector entities, not just Central Government, became required to receive and process e-invoices.</p>
<p>This B2G foundation matters beyond its own scope, since it''s the infrastructure Ireland''s much larger forthcoming domestic B2B mandate will build directly on top of — the same Peppol network, the same EN 16931 standard, and the same OGP-Revenue governance split, just extended to private-sector trading relationships rather than only public procurement.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> if you supply Irish Central Government or public bodies, this has been a settled requirement for years, not a new one — confirm your existing Peppol connectivity is genuinely still functioning correctly rather than assuming a years-old setup remains fine untested. Given this same infrastructure underpins the B2B mandate now being phased in, any gaps in your current B2G Peppol setup are worth resolving now, before the domestic B2B rollout adds real commercial pressure on top.</p>');
INSERT INTO stories (id, date, month, summary_en, html_en, source_url, published) VALUES ('2025-10-08-ireland-b2b-mandate-announced', '2025-10-08', '2025-10', 'As part of Budget 2026, Ireland''s Revenue Commissioners announced a three-phase domestic B2B e-invoicing and real-time reporting mandate — large corporates from November 2028, all VAT-registered businesses in intra-EU trade from November 2029, converging with the EU''s own July 2030 ViDA deadline.', '<h3>🇮🇪 Revenue announces phased domestic B2B e-invoicing mandate, deliberately ahead of the EU''s own deadline</h3>
<p>Following a public consultation that ran from October 2023 to January 2024, Revenue set out a genuinely deliberate strategic choice: rather than waiting for the EU''s own 1 July 2030 ViDA cross-border deadline and absorbing all the change at once, Ireland is running a domestic-first phased rollout. Phase 1, from November 2028, brings large corporates into mandatory e-invoicing and real-time reporting for domestic B2B transactions. Phase 2, from November 2029, extends this to all VAT-registered businesses engaged in intra-EU cross-border trade. Phase 3, from July 2030, converges with the EU-wide ViDA framework itself.</p>
<p>The architecture is already decided, not left vague: EN 16931-compliant structured e-invoices, exchanged through the Peppol network on a five-corner model, with selected transaction data reported to Revenue close to real time. Worth noting directly: this changes invoicing and reporting processes specifically — VAT rates, payment requirements, and liability calculations themselves remain entirely unchanged. From November 2028 onward, every business will also need the capability to receive e-invoices, regardless of whether they''re yet obligated to issue them themselves.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> November 2028 is genuinely distant, but the phased structure itself is worth planning around now rather than later — if your business might later qualify as a "large corporate," Revenue has already signalled detailed technical specifications and testing guidance will arrive well before each phase, giving real lead time if you start tracking it now. Given Ireland is deliberately moving ahead of the EU''s own cross-border deadline rather than waiting for it, this is one of the more disciplined, clearly-sequenced ViDA-alignment approaches in this tracker.</p>', 'https://www.revenue.ie/en/vat/vida-vat-modernisation/index.aspx', 1);
INSERT INTO story_countries (story_id, country_id) SELECT '2025-10-08-ireland-b2b-mandate-announced', id FROM countries WHERE name_en = 'Ireland';
INSERT INTO story_translations (story_id, lang, title, summary, html) VALUES ('2025-10-08-ireland-b2b-mandate-announced', 'en', 'Revenue announces phased domestic B2B e-invoicing mandate, deliberately ahead of the EU''s own deadline', 'As part of Budget 2026, Ireland''s Revenue Commissioners announced a three-phase domestic B2B e-invoicing and real-time reporting mandate — large corporates from November 2028, all VAT-registered businesses in intra-EU trade from November 2029, converging with the EU''s own July 2030 ViDA deadline.', '<h3>🇮🇪 Revenue announces phased domestic B2B e-invoicing mandate, deliberately ahead of the EU''s own deadline</h3>
<p>Following a public consultation that ran from October 2023 to January 2024, Revenue set out a genuinely deliberate strategic choice: rather than waiting for the EU''s own 1 July 2030 ViDA cross-border deadline and absorbing all the change at once, Ireland is running a domestic-first phased rollout. Phase 1, from November 2028, brings large corporates into mandatory e-invoicing and real-time reporting for domestic B2B transactions. Phase 2, from November 2029, extends this to all VAT-registered businesses engaged in intra-EU cross-border trade. Phase 3, from July 2030, converges with the EU-wide ViDA framework itself.</p>
<p>The architecture is already decided, not left vague: EN 16931-compliant structured e-invoices, exchanged through the Peppol network on a five-corner model, with selected transaction data reported to Revenue close to real time. Worth noting directly: this changes invoicing and reporting processes specifically — VAT rates, payment requirements, and liability calculations themselves remain entirely unchanged. From November 2028 onward, every business will also need the capability to receive e-invoices, regardless of whether they''re yet obligated to issue them themselves.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> November 2028 is genuinely distant, but the phased structure itself is worth planning around now rather than later — if your business might later qualify as a "large corporate," Revenue has already signalled detailed technical specifications and testing guidance will arrive well before each phase, giving real lead time if you start tracking it now. Given Ireland is deliberately moving ahead of the EU''s own cross-border deadline rather than waiting for it, this is one of the more disciplined, clearly-sequenced ViDA-alignment approaches in this tracker.</p>');
INSERT INTO stories (id, date, month, summary_en, html_en, source_url, published) VALUES ('2026-02-10-ireland-large-corporate-criteria', '2026-02-10', '2026-02', 'Revenue has confirmed that Phase One''s ''large corporate'' definition is based on being managed by Revenue''s Large Corporates Division with a fixed Irish establishment — an administrative test, not simply a turnover threshold — with affected businesses to receive direct written notification.', '<h3>🇮🇪 Revenue confirms who counts as a ''large corporate'' for Phase One — and it''s administrative, not just financial</h3>
<p>One of the most operationally important open questions from October 2025''s implementation announcement has now been resolved: Revenue has confirmed exactly who counts as a "large corporate" for Phase One of the domestic B2B mandate. The definition is genuinely administrative rather than purely financial — a business qualifies if its tax affairs are managed by Revenue''s Large Corporates Division and it maintains a fixed establishment in Ireland. This matters because it means eligibility isn''t something a business can simply calculate from its own turnover figures; it depends on how Revenue itself already classifies the taxpayer internally.</p>
<p>Revenue has also been unambiguous on a separate but related point worth flagging directly: PDFs and scanned paper invoices are explicitly not e-invoices for compliance purposes under this regime, regardless of how digital they might feel in practice. Businesses identified as in-scope for Phase One can expect direct written notification from Revenue confirming their inclusion, rather than needing to self-assess and register proactively.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> don''t try to determine your Phase One status purely from your own revenue figures — if you''re unsure whether Revenue''s Large Corporates Division already manages your tax affairs, that''s the actual question to resolve, not a turnover calculation. And if any part of your current invoicing process relies on PDF or scanned documents, treat that as something that will need to change under this mandate regardless of format sophistication, since Revenue has been explicit that neither counts as compliant.</p>', 'https://www.revenue.ie/en/vat/vida-vat-modernisation/index.aspx', 1);
INSERT INTO story_countries (story_id, country_id) SELECT '2026-02-10-ireland-large-corporate-criteria', id FROM countries WHERE name_en = 'Ireland';
